The scheduled Sunday run is complete and committed. Here's what it found and produced.

## The pick

The category's most-used proof claim — "science-backed" — is asserted at the **ingredient** level, while evidence, regulators, and buyers all operate one to four levels lower. Today gave that a same-day primary receipt: the operator of a supplement-evidence site published a full methodological self-audit on r/Biohackers. He had scored 2,156 supplement-goal pairings against published trials, put a 22% "no evidence" figure behind a DOI, then went back and checked. Of the 475 pairings his pipeline scored zero, **277 had at least one real RCT and 66 had ten or more**. Protein for osteoporosis, scored zero, has 104.

Two causes, both his instrument: the pipeline searched a capped 200-record sample per supplement instead of querying per pairing, and its deduplication left Citrulline and L-Citrulline as separate compounds scoring 0 and 9 for the same use.

The turn that makes it Farrice's rather than a compliance blog's: **absence claims are structurally weaker than presence claims, and a spreadsheet renders them identically.** When a brand's claims review comes back thin, nobody asks whether the search was broken — they soften the copy. Caution and emptiness read the same to a buyer, so the brand pays twice: once for the study it already owned, once for the sentence it was too careful to write.

## What shipped

Two finished assets, both filed to the vault as READY: the **flagship LinkedIn post** (274 words, opens on the number and the man who disproved it) and the Sunday rotating format, a **six-rung education long-form** walking evidence from cell → ingredient → goal → dose → form/population → finished formula. Plus hook stack, carousel outline, video script, teardown prompt, and DM angle as seeds.

Living docs were fed: doc 07 gained a reactivity read (this audience rewards published errors over polished conclusions), doc 03 gained a new buying trigger (the thin claims review). Three rows to each ledger, JSONL validated clean.

## Gates and honesty notes

Everything passed, but not on the first try, and two things are worth your eye:

The education long-form **failed** `content_finish_gate` on an "It's not X. It's Y." reveal in rung one and **warned** on `prose_classifier` for parallel structure across six consecutive rung paragraphs. I fixed both — the parallel-structure fix meant adding the escalation beats the ladder was actually missing, so the piece got better rather than just compliant. Both re-checked CLEAN.

`chain_runner finalize` returned composite 6.67 with intent_alignment at 4.0. That is the same specialized-lane mismatch yesterday's run logged: the scorer grades against a generic content-deliverable rubric and reads a scheduled system run as off-intent. Reported, not chased.

One discrepancy I found and did **not** silently fix: yesterday's brief states no Drive write was attempted, but a Doc titled "2026-08-01 — Angle Map Listening Brief" exists in the approved folder, created at 13:01Z — the scheduled-run slot, not the later Codex session. Most likely the scheduled run exported and the Codex run that authored the brief didn't know. I flagged it in today's brief and left yesterday's dated record untouched.

Today's Drive mirror is live and verified by exporting the Doc back to plain text and comparing: [2026-08-02 — Angle Map Listening Brief](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1J8gXFRYR_yubTZbcuQuEZPFpvsgAsXZDB9Lv5WkfRSE/edit). Apify spend was $0.042 today, $0.213 month-to-date against $29. Creatine was marked SATURATED after two consecutive briefs and appears nowhere; 14 of 15 candidate signals were non-GLP-1.

